 ## Controller
 
+### r6 opt-in, capture-local node counterparts
+
+Ordinary `capture()` and authority wire protocol v2 are unchanged. The separate
+r6 successor adds `capture({ trackNodeReferences: true })`, returning a
+`SharedTreeAuthorityReferenceCapture`. It does not add fields to the document schema.
+
+```ts
+const cut = authority.capture({ trackNodeReferences: true });
+const prefixDraft = authority.createDraft(cut, { afterPrefix: k });
+const prefixView = prefixDraft.viewWith(configuration);
+const source = cut.reference(liveNode);
+if (source.kind === "tracked") {
+    const counterpart = cut.resolve(source.reference, prefixDraft);
+    if (counterpart.kind === "node") {
+        // counterpart.node belongs to prefixView's checkout, not the live checkout.
+        // Narrow its schema with Tree.is before making typed corrections.
+    }
+}
+```
+
+`reference(node)` accepts nodes from this controller's live view or an active
+unpublished draft belonging to this exact capture. It returns `tracked` with an
+opaque object reference, or `outsideCapture` for unhydrated nodes and births
+outside the captured sequenced history. A local unsequenced birth stays outside
+that old capture even after its Fluid acknowledgement. Correction-draft-born
+node -> eventual-live correspondence is deliberately unsupported; numeric native
+IDs, matching contents and schema identifiers cannot substitute for it.
+
+`resolve(reference, "live" | draft)` returns:
+
+* `node`, with the target checkout's wrapper and `inDocument` or `removed` status;
+* `absent`, when the node is absent from that historical projection, including
+  a prefix before its creation;
+* `unavailable` / `destroyed`, if a previously represented counterpart was destroyed.
+
+`removed` can also describe a descendant of a removed tree; existing reinsertion
+rules still admit only supported removed roots in the same checkout. Wrong
+captures/controllers/checkouts, forged references, sealed/disposed drafts,
+expired captures, missing/incompatible target views, unfinished transactions
+and transient change-event access are usage errors. Dispose the capture to
+release its anchors and all its reference-enabled draft checkouts.
+
+References are established once at an actual forest-clone boundary and then
+follow real delta visitors. The tracked S snapshot is projected from live state
+by rolling back L. Each tracked prefix draft forks S directly, before rolling
+back the excluded P suffix; it does not pass through A and recreate P-born nodes.
+Detached-root IDs are seeded from `DetachedFieldIndex`. Anchor IDs and wrapper
+caches are not shared between forests. Paths are used only as coordinates at the
+verified clone boundary, never for subsequent counterpart lookup or recovery.
+
+The opt-in eagerly anchors all stored nodes, including leaves and retained
+detached content. Defaults are `maxNodes: 10000`, `maxDrafts: 16` concurrently
+retained public reference drafts, and a fixed maximum node depth of 256.
+Optional positive safe-integer node/draft limits can be supplied. Exceeding a
+limit fails rather than truncating coverage. Space is O(nodes * retained views);
+this is not a production-scale lazy index. The node limit includes the temporary
+local snapshot, even though L-born nodes cannot yield public references.
+The enrollment limit does not cap subsequent live/draft edits, new ancestor
+paths, or the underlying retained history; there is no implicit eviction.
+
+References are valid only in this process/controller/capture lifetime. There is
+no token serialization, original-cut reopening, global identity, or durable
+native UUID sidecar contract. Existing authority-mode unbounded history retention
+is still required; holding an anchor alone does not pin repair data against
+destruction. All r5 summary/recovery and detached-content restrictions below remain.
+
+### Existing authority operations
+
